Performance Testing Phase Workflow – Educational Management System

Objective:
To evaluate how efficiently the system performs under different loads and conditions, ensuring that the Educational Management System is reliable, fast, and scalable.

⸻

1. Identify Performance Criteria
	•	Response Time: Time taken to load pages, submit forms, or retrieve data.
	•	Throughput: Number of transactions handled per second (e.g., student data retrieval, admission submissions).
	•	Resource Utilization: CPU, memory, and network usage under load.
	•	Scalability: System behavior when the number of users increases.

⸻

2. Prepare Test Environment
	•	Set up a test server similar to the production environment.
	•	Use a test database with realistic sample data.
	•	Configure monitoring tools to capture performance metrics.

⸻

3. Define Test Scenarios

Examples of key scenarios for the Educational Management System:
	1.	Concurrent Logins: Multiple students, teachers, and administrators logging in simultaneously.
	2.	Data Retrieval: Fetching student records, academic progress, and reports.
	3.	Form Submission: Admissions, attendance, and feedback forms submitted simultaneously.
	4.	Workflow Automation: Triggering notifications, approvals, and report generation under load.

⸻

4. Execute Performance Tests
	•	Use tools like JMeter, LoadRunner, or Apache Benchmark.
	•	Simulate real-world scenarios with multiple users.
	•	Gradually increase load to measure system response and stability.

⸻

5. Monitor & Collect Metrics
	•	Response Time: Ensure it meets acceptable limits.
	•	Throughput: Check if the system can handle expected user load.
	•	Error Rates: Record failures or timeouts.
	•	Resource Utilization: Monitor CPU, memory, and database performance.

⸻

6. Analyze Results
	•	Compare results against expected performance criteria.
	•	Identify bottlenecks in server, database, or application logic.
	•	Highlight scenarios where performance degrades under load.

⸻

7. Optimize & Retest
	•	Improve code, database queries, or server configuration based on findings.
	•	Retest to verify improvements.
	•	Repeat until performance is within acceptable limits.

⸻

8. Document Findings
	•	Summarize test scenarios, results, and optimizations.
	•	Provide performance graphs, tables, and recommendations for future scalability.
